# LumOn Surface Cache Lighting Integration and Test Coverage

## 1. Dynamic lighting history
- [x] Add a reusable controlled-scene test for removing and restoring light after the surface cache has accumulated illumination.
- [x] Verify production history invalidation removes stale illumination, unresolved geometry adds no samples, and one complete page update produces the new lighting; unchanged inputs retain accumulation.
- [x] Extend the fixture with doorway closure/reopening and verify stale illumination disappears within a defined update budget.

Verified by `SurfaceCacheDynamicLightingTests.LightRemovalAndRestorationDiscardAccumulatedHistory`: four initial bright samples, light off/on, all 64 texels checked for RGB and sample weight. Each settled transition uses one page update with one ray per texel and a 256-step traversal limit. This exercises shared geometry publication and the production history-reset owner, not the complete renderer lifecycle or its page requeue orchestration.

`SurfaceCacheDynamicLightingTests.DoorwayClosureAndReopeningInvalidateIllumination` covers a dark enclosed room with fixed bright exterior lighting. Only the doorway occupancy changes; GPU readback verifies the door changes and the captured back wall remains unchanged. After four accumulated open-door samples, closing the door clears history and produces valid dark samples across all 64 texels. Reopening restores the original attenuated RGB pattern. Unpublished geometry must fail relighting without adding sample weight. Each transition settles within one complete page update after publication, using one ray per texel and a 256-step traversal limit.

Validation: normal SPIR-V-enabled build passed; both dynamic-history cases and existing shared-surface tests passed 13/13 with no skips. Receipts: `artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/surface-cache-doorway-finalv2-build.log` and `surface-cache-doorway-finalv2.trx` in the same directory.

## 2. Full runtime orchestration
- [x] Exercise the production surface-cache renderer lifecycle: initialization, feedback, allocation, capture, relighting, debug-view activation, and disposal.
- [x] Verify published resources and rendered output across multiple frames, including resource recreation, without OpenGL errors.

Verified by `SurfaceCacheRuntimeTests.RegisteredCallbacksDriveSurfaceCacheAcrossRecreationAndWorldRestart`, which drives the registered production callbacks through controlled engine boundaries. The reusable fixture publishes controlled geometry before submitting feedback, then verifies allocation, successful capture and relight completion, debug-view callback execution, physical-atlas recreation, world teardown and restart, and callback cleanup. `DisposedRenderersRemoveTheirEngineCallbacks` independently guards renderer unregistration, while `RuntimePublishedGeometrySupportsSurfaceCapture` correlates runtime-published geometry with both the established source-compiled capture fixture and the production SPIR-V capture owner.

The runtime fixture intentionally treats nonzero final lighting as separate coverage. This section proves that a resident page becomes sampleable and survives the required lifecycle transitions; Section 7 owns proof that cached lighting reaches final indirect-lighting pixels.

Validation: normal incremental build passed; `SurfaceCacheRuntimeTests` passed 3/3 with no skips. Isolated neighboring runs also passed: `SurfaceCacheDynamicLightingTests` 2/2 and `SharedTraceSceneSurfaceTests` 11/11. Receipts: `artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/surface-cache-runtime-current-v40-tests.log`, `surface-cache-dynamic-isolated-tests.log`, and `surface-cache-shared-surface-isolated-tests.log`, with their corresponding `.trx` files.

## 3. Surface-cache consumer audit

Status: historical consumer audit complete; hit-consumer implementation is tracked in Section 6 and full behavioral verification is recorded in Section 7.

- [x] Identify the currently implemented surface-cache consumers and explicitly record any missing integration before defining end-to-end expectations.

The original audit found only a diagnostic surface-cache consumer. Production screen/world-probe hit lighting had no connection to the cache. Section 6 now implements that connection and replaces the missing-integration assertion with positive hit-consumer coverage. Gather and final composition remain downstream of probe radiance and do not bind the surface cache directly.

The illumination/removal proof across filtering, gather and final pixels is recorded in Section 7. The validation below records the original audit, not that later end-to-end behavior.
Validation: the focused consumer-contract run passed 2/2 with no failures or skips and built all 98 shader stages / 243 SPIR-V variants. Receipts: `artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/surface-cache-consumer-contract-tests.log` and `surface-cache-consumer-contract.trx`.

Use modular fixtures shared across scenarios. Record executed tests and results when completing each item.

## 4. Surface lighting terms and storage contract

Controlling design and task traceability: [Surface-cache lighting contract](LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md). Its storage design governs Sections 5–7; Section 4 implements the estimator corrections without claiming the later producers or consumers exist.

- [x] Define separate direct irradiance, indirect irradiance, linear diffuse albedo, and emitted radiance terms. Specify units, exposure convention, sky/block-light classification, and normalization for every producer and consumer.
- [x] Define cached outgoing diffuse radiance as `L = (E_direct + E_indirect) * diffuseAlbedo / pi + emission`, with finite, nonnegative output. Keep irradiance distinct from material-modulated outgoing radiance in names, resources, and debug views.
- [x] Audit the existing relight estimator, including angular/PDF weighting and `1 / (1 + distance^2)` attenuation; replace incompatible behavior and old test expectations with the documented lighting contract.
- [x] Specify atlas layers, per-term validity/history, and publication ownership for direct irradiance, indirect irradiance, and combined outgoing radiance. Preserve bounded allocation and update budgets.

Validation: second implementation review and independent completion audit passed. The normal shader-enabled build compiled 98 stages / 243 SPIR-V variants. Focused GPU suites passed 18/18 with zero failures or skips: lighting terms 5/5, dynamic history 2/2, shared surface 11/11. Receipts: artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/surface-cache-terms-{lighting,dynamic,shared}.{log,trx}. New coverage checks distance invariance, nonwhite hit reflectance, rejection of incomplete hemisphere batches, and capped-history energy stability.

## 5. Produce and publish complete surface lighting

Controlling design and task traceability: [Surface-cache lighting contract](LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md).
- [x] Capture the material data needed for diffuse reflectance and emission; compute direct irradiance from supported scene lighting with the documented visibility and sky treatment.
- [x] Update indirect irradiance by tracing against previously published surface outgoing radiance, with correct integration weights and bounded temporal accumulation. Prevent same-dispatch read/write feedback and double-counting direct light or emission.
- [x] Combine the lighting terms into outgoing radiance and publish coherent page mappings, generations, readiness, and lighting resources through a read-only provider.
- [x] Invalidate affected lighting and dependent indirect history when lights, materials, geometry, residency, or atlas resources change; verify bounded convergence after light removal and restoration.
- [x] Add reusable numerical tests for isolated direct, indirect, and emissive terms, albedo scaling, normalization, valid darkness, and stable multi-bounce updates.

Validation: second implementation review and independent completion audit passed. Normal shader-enabled build compiled 99 stages / 244 variants. Focused suites passed 44/44 with zero failures or skips: producers 7, runtime 5, pool/budget units 10, dynamic history 2, shared surface 11, estimator terms 5, debug bindings 4. Producer coverage uses real capture and previous-generation transport, checks every page completion marker and outgoing texel validity, and verifies direct/albedo/emission accounting, normalization, unavailable-page history, removal/restoration and stable multiple bounces. The runtime test verifies partial-page readiness and bitwise preservation of a previously lit tile across publication swaps, plus configuration/resource invalidation. Receipts: artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/surface-lighting-build.log, surface-lighting-producer.trx, surface-lighting-runtime-final.{log,trx}, surface-lighting-pool.{log,trx}, and surface-lighting-{SurfaceCacheDynamicLightingTests,SharedTraceSceneSurfaceTests,SurfaceCacheLightingTermsTests,SurfaceCacheDebugBindingTests}.{log,trx}.

Provider production is complete. Section 6 owns screen/world-probe consumption; Section 7 owns final-lighting proof.

## 6. Consume surface lighting at geometry hits

Controlling design and task traceability: [Surface-cache lighting contract](LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md), especially Geometry-hit consumers and Atlas and publication design.
- [x] Implement shared hit-to-surface addressing from geometry hit position, face/material identity, and chunk generation through the page table to cached outgoing radiance. Test cross-chunk hits, boundaries, stale mappings, and unavailable pages.
- [x] Connect the published provider to `LumOnRenderer` and the screen-probe near-field hit-lighting path through shader-owned contracts. Preserve screen-hit behavior; consume cached outgoing radiance for supported world-geometry hits without applying hit albedo twice.
- [x] Define explicit unavailable-cache behavior separately from valid black lighting, and preserve hit distance, occlusion, confidence, and history semantics.
- [x] Connect world-probe trace hit lighting to the same surface-lighting contract. Resolve the current CPU-worker/GPU-cache ownership boundary explicitly; do not synchronously read GPU textures from tracing workers. Record any required GPU tracing or snapshot transport work as an implementation dependency.
- [x] Keep gather and final composition downstream of probe radiance. Reserve reflection integration for an actual reflection consumer rather than introducing an unused pipeline.

Validation: second implementation review and independent completion audit passed. Normal shader-enabled build compiled 100 stages / 245 variants. Focused suites passed 104 distinct tests with zero failures or skips: hit consumers 12, near-field behavior 57, world-probe transport 2, scheduler 5, runtime publication 5, shared surface 11, producers 7, shader bindings 3, consumer contracts 2. Coverage includes real produced radiance at screen and CPU-world-probe hits, accepted screen-hit emission exactly once, signed cross-chunk addressing, stale/aliased/unavailable pages, valid black, preserved hit distances, query-buffer reuse, dependency revisions and delayed-request rejection.

World-probe geometry tracing remains on workers. The implemented dependency is bounded asynchronous render-thread GPU hit resolution (at most 4,096 descriptors), followed by whole-probe atlas upload. Workers perform no GPU readback. Hits outside resident cache coverage remain unresolved. Final-pixel and multi-frame consumer proof remain Section 7; no live visual verification is claimed.

Receipts: artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/surface-consumer-build-confirmed.log; surface-consumer-{lookup,runtime,bindings,contract}-verified.{log,trx}; surface-consumer-{nearfield,world,scheduler}-current.{log,trx}; surface-consumer-shared-final.{log,trx}; surface-consumer-producer.{log,trx}.
## 7. End-to-end lighting proof

Controlling design and traceability: [Surface-cache lighting contract](LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md), including Final-lighting verification traceability.

- [x] Replace `FinalLightingShadersDoNotYetDeclareSurfaceCacheInputs` with positive consumer coverage; absence of integration must not remain a required behavior.
- [x] Trace controlled scene lighting changes through real cache production, hit sampling, probe radiance, filtering, both gather modes, and final indirect-lighting pixels. Verify illumination, removal, and restoration without injecting downstream lighting.
- [x] Cover screen misses with offscreen geometry, emissive-only illumination, sealed-room darkness, multi-bounce transfer, missing/stale pages, and resource recreation. Check intermediate radiance as well as final pixels to identify the failing boundary.
- [x] Extend world-probe coverage through its implemented cache consumer and exercise runtime publication/invalidation across frames. Record build and executed GPU-test evidence separately from live visual verification.

Validation: second implementation review and independent completion audit passed. The normal shader-enabled build compiled 100 stages / 245 variants; the final incremental build confirmed binaries and contracts current with zero errors (five existing analyzer warnings). GPU/contract suites passed 45 distinct tests with zero failures or skips: final pixels 14, world-probe flow 5, runtime publication 5, producers 7, hit consumers 12, consumer contracts 2.

The reusable downstream fixture passes actual traced GPU attachments through filtering, direct atlas or SH9 gather, upsample and final material composition. Tests change only source lighting/material policy or cache availability; no downstream illumination is injected. Both paths cover removal/restoration, emission, sealed darkness with bright exterior fields, successive bounces, missing/stale pages and recreated resources. World-probe coverage includes CPU hits, GPU queries, actual directional atlas uploads, final pixels and registered runtime cache transitions.

The runtime transition test exposed and now guards a recapture stall: invalidated pages were marked but never scheduled, and failed captures had no retry. The feedback owner now requests recapture after invalidation and retains unresolved pages for later bounded sweeps. A separate case observes the GPU failure marker before enabling geometry, then verifies recovery without allocating a replacement page.

Receipts in artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/: surface-final-build-initial.log; surface-final-build-verified.log; surface-final-{world,runtime}-verified.{log,trx}; surface-final-{pixels,producer,consumer,contract}-validated.{log,trx}. Original failing receipts remain available. This is headless GPU evidence; no live in-game visual verification is claimed.

Implementation order: 4 → 5 → 6 → 7 → 8 → 9 → 10 → 11 → 12. Sections 4–6 own production integration; Section 7 owns the behavioral verification moved from Section 3. Missing consumer implementation must remain distinct from test coverage, and injected downstream lighting cannot substitute for cache consumption. Existing Sections 1–2 remain historical validation of the earlier estimator and lifecycle, and their numerical expectations must be updated where the lighting contract changes.

## Follow-up coverage requirements

Section 7 proves controlled cache-to-pixel transport, not the complete live pipeline across frames. Its fixture skips temporal accumulation, supplies fixed anchors, connects consumer components manually, and ends with `lumon_combine`. Section 8 improves shader-build throughput before Sections 9–12 close these remaining gaps without weakening the completed tests.

Controlling design for the lighting coverage tasks in Sections 9–12: [Surface-cache lighting contract](LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md), especially Lighting terms and units, Indirect estimator and history, Atlas and publication design, and Geometry-hit consumers. Reuse modular fixtures and real production shaders/owners; change controlled scene inputs rather than injecting downstream radiance. Exercise both gather modes where applicable. Define numerical tolerances and update budgets before evaluating results, check intermediate outputs and final pixels, and record executed build/GPU-test receipts separately from any live visual verification.

## 8. Parallel SPIR-V shader compilation

- [x] Profile the current shader-build pipeline and record a reproducible serial baseline: elapsed build time, compiled stage/variant counts, and preprocessing versus external compiler time. Identify shared mutable state, output ownership and build-target ordering before introducing concurrency.
- [x] Refactor independent stage/variant compilation into bounded concurrent jobs, using worker threads and/or multiple compiler processes as appropriate. Expose a configurable concurrency limit with a serial mode; preserve TinyAst preprocessing, shader-owned contracts, variant selection and the existing build entry points.
- [x] Isolate per-job preprocessing state, temporary files and compiler outputs. Complete artifact cleanup before scheduling jobs, publish only successful artifacts, and prevent duplicate jobs or simultaneous builds from racing over shared output paths.
- [x] Preserve deterministic artifact naming and diagnostics tied to their shader/stage/variant. Propagate compiler failures and cancellation to the build, stop outstanding work safely, and ensure failed jobs cannot leave apparently successful artifacts or orphan compiler processes.
- [x] Add reusable tests for serial/parallel artifact equivalence, concurrency limits, output isolation, deterministic diagnostic reporting and failure/cancellation handling. Verify the normal application/test build still compiles all required shader variants before consumption.
- [x] Compare serial and parallel clean builds under matched conditions with identical stage/variant counts and correctness checks. Record repeated timings, peak resource usage and the chosen default concurrency; separate incremental-build results from full compilation.

Completion: the normal build uses bounded parallel compilation, preserves artifact and failure behavior, and has measured shader-build speedup over the retained serial mode. Record executed tests and benchmark receipts; successful concurrent execution alone does not establish a performance improvement.

Traceability: all six tasks are governed by this section; it links no additional controlling design document. The lighting-contract reference above explicitly governs Sections 9–12. Tasks 1 and 6 require matched production-catalog serial/parallel timing, work counts, per-step timing and resource receipts. Tasks 2–4 require bounded job/process ownership, preserved TinyAst/contract emission, exclusive output cleanup/publication, ordered diagnostics and joined cancellation. Task 5 requires scheduler/process/entry-point tests, byte-identical serial/parallel artifacts and the normal shader-enabled consumer build. Planned evidence: `ShaderBuildTool.Tests`, the existing `ShaderBuildTool/Tests/ValidateBuildContract.ps1`, and receipts under `artifacts/spirv-parallel/`. Second implementation review and the independent completion audit both passed; all task requirements have observed evidence.
Implementation controls: the build tool accepts `--concurrency N`; the normal MSBuild entry point accepts `-p:SpirvConcurrency=N`. The default is `min(8, Environment.ProcessorCount)`, with `1` retaining serial execution. Source imports expand once before workers start; each job creates its own editable AST and pending compiler output. An adjacent output lease excludes competing writers before cleanup or incremental validation. Completed diagnostics are reported in catalog order, failed jobs cancel peers and prevent a success receipt, and compiler cancellation terminates the child process tree. Timing output separates source expansion, summed variant-emission work, summed compiler-process work and total elapsed time; summed work overlaps during parallel runs and is not elapsed time.

Measured clean-build comparison (same production inputs, output paths, pinned compiler and frozen candidate tool; order serial/parallel/parallel/serial on an i9-12900K with 24 logical processors):

| Run | Compiler jobs | Elapsed seconds | Peak sampled process-tree working set (MiB) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| A1 | 1 | 155.449 | 537.15 |
| B1 | 8 | 34.213 | 1381.49 |
| B2 | 8 | 34.174 | 1096.27 |
| A2 | 1 | 145.128 | 534.52 |

All runs compiled 100 stages / 245 variants. Mean elapsed time fell from 150.289 to 34.193 seconds (4.395x, 77.25% lower). All 245 binaries matched byte-for-byte across both original serial baselines and all four candidate runs. The original frozen compiler took 142.039 and 154.529 seconds; only the matched candidate runs above support the reported ratio. Retained serial profiling measured 2.132–2.751 seconds of source expansion, 15.330–16.016 seconds of variant emission and 126.941–135.083 seconds in compiler processes. Eight workers are the measured default cap, not a claim of optimal concurrency on every machine. Memory is an approximate process-tree working-set peak sampled at roughly 500 ms, not an allocation bound or exact peak; short-lived processes and PID ancestry can affect it. The scheduler tests independently verify the job limit. Incremental no-op behavior is verified separately and excluded from the clean-build speedup.

Timing, count, resource and hash receipts: `artifacts/spirv-parallel/{baseline-1,baseline-2,candidate-a1,candidate-b1,candidate-b2,candidate-a2}.{log,json}`, corresponding `*-hashes.csv`, and `comparison.json`. The existing build-contract validator passed: `artifacts/spirv-build-contract/run-20260923-102229/{result.txt,receipts.json}`. Final compiler tests passed 14/14 with no skips or warnings (`artifacts/spirv-parallel/tests-final.log`, `parallel-tests-final.trx`). The normal shader-enabled application/test build passed with zero errors, default jobs=8 and all 100 stages / 245 variants (`consumer-build.log`); five existing unrelated test-analyzer warnings remain. The MSBuild serial override passed with zero warnings/errors and a verified incremental no-op (`consumer-override.log`). All 245 test-output binaries match the producer hashes. These consumer builds used isolated output directories so the running game remained untouched. Second implementation review and independent completion audit passed with no outstanding findings; this section is complete.

## 9. Persistent temporal history

- [ ] Extend the cache-to-pixel fixture with production temporal accumulation, retained probe history and partial directional updates across frames. Verify unchanged inputs preserve accumulation rather than resetting each frame.
- [ ] Accumulate bright lighting, remove and restore its source, and verify stale illumination disappears and lighting returns within a defined frame/update budget through both gather modes.
- [ ] Exercise cache unavailability, dependency revision changes and resource recreation with populated history. Verify invalidation rejects obsolete lighting while ordinary bounce-generation publication preserves valid history.
- [ ] Extend the controlled doorway fixture through final pixels: accumulate open-door illumination, close the doorway with the exterior still bright, then reopen it. Assert occlusion, disappearance of stale lighting and restoration across frames.

Completion: multi-frame GPU tests demonstrate correct history retention and rejection; rebuilding an empty pipeline for each observation does not satisfy this section.

## 10. Full consumer runtime orchestration

- [ ] Extend the runtime fixture to drive `LumOnRenderer` and the world-probe update renderer alongside the production geometry/cache owners through registered callbacks and real provider wiring. Include production anchor generation, tracing, temporal processing, filtering, gather and final output.
- [ ] Exercise actual worker scheduling, delayed CPU completion, pending GPU queries and bounded upload admission. Invalidate or replace dependencies while work is pending; prove obsolete results cannot repopulate cleared atlases or final pixels.
- [ ] Run illumination/removal/restoration, resource recreation, world teardown and restart through the composed runtime. Verify callback ordering, history/atlas resets, republishing and finite final output without OpenGL errors.

Completion: the runtime owns consumer scheduling and invalidation end to end; manually calling the integrator, resolver and uploader in test order is supplementary evidence only.

## 11. Movement, streaming and mixed probe contribution

- [ ] Move and rotate the controlled camera, including view bobbing, while observing fixed world geometry. Verify lighting and debug-independent final output stay aligned with world surfaces using corresponding world positions or a defined reference view.
- [ ] Cross positive and negative chunk boundaries, wrap geometry/probe storage, unload/reload cells and move cache coverage. Verify overlap continuity, rejection of reused stale slots and bounded recovery after newly resident data publishes.
- [ ] Enable screen and world probes together, including gather fallback. Transition geometry between visible and offscreen states and vary screen-probe confidence through actual tracing/availability changes; verify contribution follows the documented confidence policy without unexplained loss or double counting.
- [ ] Use bright and dark neighboring regions separated by a wall to check per-pixel leakage and visibility discontinuities during movement, streaming and confidence transitions in both gather modes.

Completion: moving, mixed-consumer tests assert spatial correspondence and transition behavior; stationary isolated-path energy checks do not satisfy this section.

## 12. Numerical final-image and PBR composition coverage

- [ ] Add analytically controlled scenes with expected per-channel lighting, receiver albedo scaling, metallic rejection and exposure behavior. Assert defined pixel regions and error bounds rather than only the maximum nonzero pixel.
- [ ] Check spatial coverage, localized leakage and channel correctness, and compare both gather modes against their documented integration conventions with explicit approximation tolerances.
- [ ] Extend the real cache-originating chain through the production PBR composition path. Isolate indirect contribution using controlled source changes or a matched disabled-light baseline; verify direct lighting, emission and diffuse material response are accounted for exactly once.
- [ ] Repeat source removal/restoration and history invalidation through PBR composition with retained runtime state. Preserve existing `lumon_combine` coverage and record the numerical and lifecycle evidence for both composition paths.

Completion: tests constrain lighting magnitude and spatial distribution through PBR composition as well as proving that some cache energy reaches a pixel.
